WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.040
Hi, my name is John of
John Bear Woodworks,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.120
located in Windber, Pennsylvania.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.843
I restore antiques and furniture.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.103
Today, I am restoring this

00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:32.511
three-mirror 1950s vanity.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.817
Since I knew that this piece

00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:40.160
had been painted over with
several coats of paint,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.160
I was just really excited

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.940
to see what was hidden under there.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.310
I start by applying a gel stripper,

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.360
and I apply that with a brush.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.070
And I like to really glob it on.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.028
You've got to get it on thick

00:00:51.028 --> 00:00:53.300
and then let it sit.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.325
It can't be dry.

00:00:54.325 --> 00:00:56.760
It has to stay wet, so it's working.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.080
And then, once you let that
sit for 15, 20 minutes,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.180
you can really start
scraping and finding out

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.330
what you have hiding
underneath your projects.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.205
I like to use just this regular

00:01:06.205 --> 00:01:07.940
run-of-the-mill putty knife.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.590
I round the edges on it a little bit

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:10.890
so it won't have any sharp edges

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.590
that will gouge the furniture,

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.980
and always just take special care,

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.296
use light pressure,

00:01:16.296 --> 00:01:19.029
not to gouge, scratch it any more.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.784
As those chemicals in the stripper

00:01:22.784 --> 00:01:24.900
sit on top of that finish,

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.260
whatever you're stripping,

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:29.253
it just liquefies that finish,

00:01:29.253 --> 00:01:31.620
and it leaves you with this sludge.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.761
I typically scrape it

00:01:32.761 --> 00:01:36.211
right into a metal old paint can.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.946
A tip I got one time

00:01:48.946 --> 00:01:50.900
from an old-timer I used to work with

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.660
is this sawdust trick.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.350
When I apply the sawdust,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.430
it soaks up all that stripper,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.060
and you're not left with
all that gooey mess on there

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.590
that you need to wipe off or anything.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.510
As I'm using this technique,

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.270
you want to make sure that
you go with the grain,

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.761
just like you would with sanding

00:02:07.761 --> 00:02:09.094
or scraping or anything.

00:02:09.094 --> 00:02:11.070
You don't want to go against that grain.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.880
Light pressure. You don't want to cause

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.883
any scratches or gouges to the wood.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.940
I'm sanding to remove any
of the remaining residue

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.960
or the scratches that
might be in that wood.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.430
And you'll really know
if there is anything left

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.550
when you go to apply the stain.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.930
I usually start sanding
most any type of wood

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.080
with a 150, it's a pretty common grit,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.360
and then I finish it up with a 220 grit.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.950
And this will help to remove
any more of the scratches

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.040
that might have been in the wood

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.430
and make it really smooth,

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.420
especially, like, any type of maple

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.786
or really dense wood

00:02:54.786 --> 00:02:57.227
is more likely to hold scratches.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.330
Next, I will just use an iron

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.470
and a wet rag on the wood's surface

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.370
to remove any of the scratches or dings.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.730
I will just move on to
some final odds and ends

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:17.380
and steps that I take to
just do some final touch-ups,

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.020
removing the scratches,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.060
removing any of the last
finish that might be remaining,

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.333
before I do that one last
final sanding before staining.

00:03:34.266 --> 00:03:35.930
I will use an oil-based stain,

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.880
that I mix this color to my preference.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.640
And I like this reddish tint.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.165
I feel like it really brings out

00:03:42.165 --> 00:03:44.860
that bird's eye maple a lot more.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.260
I typically will tell people

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.870
when I'm working on a piece

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.850
the red mahogany tends to look

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.053
a little bit more elegant or refined.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.317
Once the stain has had a chance to set up

00:03:55.317 --> 00:03:57.330
and dry for a couple days,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.210
I apply the seal coat of
lacquer before I sand that

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.083
and apply the final topcoat.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.145
It's always nice to see these pieces

00:04:15.145 --> 00:04:17.378
come back to life for a second life

00:04:17.378 --> 00:04:19.470
instead of ending up in the dump

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.580
or out to a curbside.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.800
And I just really enjoy this work,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.890
and it brings me happiness,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.523
and it's good to see that
it makes other people happy.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.923
He's my handy helper.

